92186045417] [17592186184421 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184421 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "45b54b13-346d-538c-876e-285764d59813" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184755 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186183139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184755 #edb/ref 1069 "q-34930" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184755 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4 \\cdot 4$ matrix and\n\n$Null(A) = Span{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}$?\n\n1. The number of non-pivot columns in $A$ is equal to $3$\n2. $Col(A) = R^{4}$\n3. $rank(A) = 2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184755 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4d185801-460b-5883-a896-8f1107477aa5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184937 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186185007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184937 #edb/ref 1061 19992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184937 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "af600efe-5480-5b61-8280-594a0825dbc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184937 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186185320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184938 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184939 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184938 #edb/ref 1061 19993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184938 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "526a000d-4f1e-513e-83a7-dcec4069077d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184938 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184939 #edb/ref 1061 19994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184939 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "10431142-f052-51d9-b9c7-38e48410c92d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186184939 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185007 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186184938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185007 #edb/ref 1061 37511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185007 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3c8115af-900b-592a-a3df-6c126d87446c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185007 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184219 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185320 #edb/ref 1064 "When we reduce a matrix to row echelon form, the number of *pivot* columns plus the number of *non-pivot* columns always equals the total number of columns of the matrix. \n\nBy translating this into the language of vector spaces, we get the **rank-nullity theorem**. The rank-nullity theorem relates the rank of a matrix to the dimension of its null space:\n\n*Let $A$ be a matrix (not necessarily square) with $n$ columns. Then,*\n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Col}(A)) + \\textrm{dim(Null}(A)) = \\textrm{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n),\n$$\n\n*or, equivalently,*\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that for some matrix $A,$ we have\n\n![](images/30/30f6472f02a4ac47f285fff05e0cdbf56367acdd18231271dc3dccf0040901bd.svg)\n\nWe can use the rank-nullity theorem to calculate $\\textrm{rank}(A).$\n\nIn this case, we must have $n=4,$ since this is the number of components in each of the vectors of the null space. \n\nAlso, we must have $\\textrm{nullity}(A)=2,$ since \n![](images/b5/b5f38590a71a2f8e1f29a5ba98920a10037737c1ebcbd3fd60f08a3e47555141.svg)\n\nTherefore, using the rank-nullity theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+2 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185320 #edb/ref 1065 3524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185320 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0728c510-110b-5bde-93ea-9050a87a6ecc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186185320 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377763 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454759 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186377763 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454760 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186377763 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186454761 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186377763 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79367" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377763 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377763 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true given that $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix with $3$ pivot columns?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n- **II.** $A$ is not  invertible\n- **III.** $\\text{nullity}(A)=3$" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186377763 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2c7ff497-8845-5d34-a5a1-e0b10d7a1e25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186377763 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,⇔\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)={0} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $A$ has $3$ pivot columns, we have $\\text{rank}(A)=3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for the matrix $A,$ the number of the columns is $n=5.$ Now, since $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 5,$ then by the invertible matrix theorem,  $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to  the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 3+\\text{nullity}(A) & =5 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =5-3 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408166 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/checkbox #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186454018 #edb/ref 1080 "false" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186454018 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186454018 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ee3ba979-73eb-55d9-98be-53eb63f15310" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186454019 #edb/ref 1080 "true" #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186454019 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186454019 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "42f7e70e-a33c-5389-83